WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.150
- I am really excited to be here today

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.540
as we continue to evolve our
special operations forces,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.703
because I really wanna hear
the thoughts and questions

00:00:14.703 --> 00:00:16.200
that this distinguished group

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.020
of both thought leaders and practitioners

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.720
and those just in the
general public that we serve,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.030
although student general public, I guess,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.430
the academic general
public that we serve, have.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.730
So I'm gonna try to leave
time for questions at the end

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.410
and then happy to talk to folks

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.170
in the intervening moments, in the breaks,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.573
in the breakout sessions,
as that's useful.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.000
As we think about SOF's
role in the 2022 NDS,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.280
I wanna start sort of towards
the end of that document,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.630
where I don't think a lot of people start,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.580
which is in the force planning section.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.170
And I'll read you the quote,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.500
but it says, the joint force
must be shaped to ensure

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.280
the ability to respond to
small-scale, short-duration crises

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.560
without substantially impacting

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.870
high-end war fighting readiness.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.140
And the reason I start there
is because that shaping

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.960
is really to us, about the continued use

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.020
and the potential growth of
our special operations forces.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.870
When you think about
what history shows us,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.080
when we are designing our
military for high-end conflict,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.040
at the end of the day,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.340
attrition based warfare does not fare well

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.980
in small scale contingencies and crises.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.100
And so that is what is in SOF's DNA,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.830
SOF was designed in World
War II to fill that niche

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.250
and then has grown since then
and evolved its capabilities.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.950
So it's part of our heritage,
it's part of our skillset.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.170
We have five core missions
as we think about them,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.790
in the sort of broader terms.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.980
One is contributing to
war fighting advantage,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.430
one is campaigning, then
there are the three Cs,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.420
counter VEO, crisis
response, and counter WMD.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.610
Those last three are what I would put

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.350
in part of what the NDS terms,

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.510
other persistent threats.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.180
SOF will continue to use its expertise

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.040
to handle those kinds of threats,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.960
so that the larger joint force

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.490
can do the first part of the NDS,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.500
to prioritize modernizing and meeting

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.390
the pacing challenge of the PRC

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.970
and dealing with the
acute threat of Russia.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.970
So when you add campaigning
into CBO, CR, as we say CM WMD,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.080
you realize that a
fundamental role for SOF,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.560
is actually also part of
the underpinning of the NDS,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.620
and that is preventing
strategic distraction.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.120
And I really think
that's an important point

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.710
for everyone to think about.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.570
When you think about the last 20 years,

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.570
when we became such experts in CT and CBO,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.030
because we're so good at crisis response.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.130
But that still meant that
the larger enterprise

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.170
really focused on that mission set

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.650
and pulled us away from some
of the evolutions that we saw

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.690
as the PRC develops with its technology,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:46.980
as we saw Russia becoming
increasingly aggressive.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.440
So with that understanding
and also I would say,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.500
with the understanding
that we do all of that,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.500
today at less than 2%,
approximately 1.7% of DODs budget.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.960
So to me, that is exactly
what we're talking about

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.720
when we say we need to have the ability

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.540
to handle those small scale contingencies

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.300
and respond to those
short duration crises,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.490
without diverting the larger joint force.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.890
The joint force can only
modernize if we are successful

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.930
in preventing those
strategic distractions.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.120
So that underpinning is there.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.500
If we then look at, what we
do in the three core NDS,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.390
directed means, how are we going to win

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.070
this decisive decade?

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.830
I wanna talk a little
bit about each of those.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.260
Integrated deterrence, campaigning,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.160
building enduring advantage.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.680
Those are our three lines
of effort under the NDS.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.270
If you think about integrated deterrence,

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.780
it is again, something that is
I think second nature to SOF.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.090
We build high functioning,
cross-functional teams

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:42.923
all the time.

00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:44.310
They are interagency most of the time,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.810
they involve foreign partners.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.990
And what we do with
that is we try to create

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.750
strategic impact with tactical excellence,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.190
but we also provide that platform

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.020
and that convening ability.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.030
That's something that carries
into integrated deterrence,

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.320
that is gonna give us options

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.510
and give our national
decision makers options

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.310
in this next decade.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.130
We also don't just convene,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.500
but we enable our interagency
partners in many cases

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.270
and our foreign partners.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.340
One really interesting example of an area

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.260
where we're trying to increase capability

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.910
and where we think there's a lot of room,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.560
is if you think about what I'll call,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.513
irregular warfare medicine.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.350
And IW medicine is about making sure

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.600
that in remote hard to reach areas,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.070
there's medical capability.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.800
That creates resiliency
for a civilian population,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.350
but also for a military
that may not be as capable,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.850
makes them more willing to fight

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.980
if they believe they have access to care

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.990
and it's particularly
urgent and acute care.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.710
So that's a good example of
where we can enable our partners

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.890
by just training them in a
very specific enabler as well.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.240
And then, as you all know,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.310
the lead up to that is we
provide some of that enabling,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.260
usually in sort of as they're learning.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.220
Another example of how we
bring everything together

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.550
and perform high functioning
cross-functional teams,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.410
is a term I think many of
you're probably familiar with,

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.270
which is the triad concept.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.340
And that is something that USASAC,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:03.780
our Army Special Forces folks,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.570
have come to come up with primarily.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:07.620
But it applies to everybody,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.230
which is the cyberspace SOF triad.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.030
And the reason that's interesting is,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.983
not because it's actually,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.980
a really different way for SOF to operate.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:17.970
It has more to do with the fact

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.710
that people don't think
of those things together.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.750
They think they're all
distinctly different, right?

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.040
Like we're supposed to be in the desert

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.030
where there is no electricity.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.580
So why would we be talking
about cyber and space, right?

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.650
How's that supposed to work?

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.020
And it's really because of
our placement and access.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.610
So our placement and access

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.460
can enable cyber and space operations.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.290
So we allow us to go to a,

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.170
we take existing capabilities,
bring them together,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.850
and then make them more
than the sum of their parts,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.500
because again, of our tactical excellence

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:44.703
in placement and access.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.150
If you think about campaigning,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.680
this is a place where I would say,

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.360
we're going a little
bit back to SOF's roots,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.760
in irregular warfare and
unconventional warfare.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.520
We traditionally have,

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.190
helped shape the operating environment.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.890
We provide indicators and
warning for the joint force

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:05.070
and that is also feeding into
integrated deterrents as well.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.780
We create dilemmas for adversaries.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.370
And this is a really interesting place,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.280
where I think in some ways
the sky is the limit for SOF,

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.800
because we are tailorable, scalable,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.230
because we can do things
at a smaller level.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:19.170
Our ability to essentially
make mischief globally,

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.210
to make our adversaries have to look left

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.970
when they wanna be staring
right, is infinite.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.340
It's really a matter of how
much we are willing to do

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:27.990
and where we're willing to do it.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.110
But our adversaries are looking globally.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.300
They're not just looking
at the INDOPACOM AOR.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.420
And so it's really important
that we be creating dilemmas

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.820
for them both globally and in INDOPACOM.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.400
And that's something that SOF
is uniquely positioned to do.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.200
And that also flows a little
bit into the indicators

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.330
and warning because we're able
to then get to another thing,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.780
which is help counter
coercion and influence.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.160
By being present,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.583
we make it harder for our
adversaries to win the day.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.040
I would say what we see big
picture, is most in the world,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.370
don't wanna have to make
a choice and that's okay.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.650
But what we don't want them
to do is choose the PRC way.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.750
We don't want them to give
the PRC unlimited access,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:07.590
unlimited rights.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.276
We want them to feel like they
have to at a minimum balance.

00:07:10.276 --> 00:07:12.810
Where possible, we want
that balance to shift to us.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.200
And by being present, in some
cases it doesn't take much,

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.150
because there is sort of a traditional,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.500
the Bellator initiative, things like that,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.190
that increasingly other countries see,

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.640
through kind of both the
cost and the benefits, right?

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.040
When you build roads and buildings

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.510
that are made with shoddy concrete

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.490
and they fall down three years later,

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.080
maybe you should have just waited,

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.430
what would probably be
the full three years

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.530
for the US government to approve
any sort of project, right?

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.210
I mean that's the dilemma.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.780
But what most countries do,
and it's the logical choice,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.150
for what I would call a
weaker state strategy,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.760
is they try to get
something from both of us,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:48.810
'cuz that's what they need.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.190
They need a lot, you know,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.570
they see themselves as needing a lot

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.740
and they'll take from both

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.600
and they'd rather not have to choose.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.630
And again, as long as we are
there on the playing field,

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.430
making sure we understand
when that balance

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:01.380
would shift to our detriment,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.240
then we can keep that
in balance at a minimum

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.970
and over time hopefully
get to our advantage.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.380
But that only happens if you're present

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.710
and only SOF can be present
in numbers globally.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.663
We are in 80 countries today.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.800
So our ability to see that coercion,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.830
to see the influence and
to adjust that balance,

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.250
we are the force that's
doing that, without big mass,

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.800
without creating huge, new
dilemmas for our host countries

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.033
and potential partners.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:29.303
The other thing that we do

00:08:29.303 --> 00:08:31.680
that I would say is very
traditional is in our mission sets

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.340
of increasing partner
resilience, resistance forces,

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.760
special reconnaissance,
and partner adaptability.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.740
Like those are all classic
missions that we've had

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.910
from our founding

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:44.670
and they're ones that
we're returning to now.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.950
When we then look at enduring advantage,

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.870
I think that's where you see a
really exciting time for SOF,

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.120
because when I talk about kind of using

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.800
some of what we already know how to do,

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.697
going back to our roots,
it's about the balancing act.

00:08:58.697 --> 00:09:01.380
And the second piece of that
is investing in our future

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.900
and investing in the now
and how we rebalance.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.880
And a big piece of that

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.880
is us starting to develop
and align with our,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.980
a joint concept for operating

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.580
that builds on service
specific capabilities

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.670
and really highlights those,
but still brings them together.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.560
And I say that because
I think most of you know

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.480
that over the last two decades,

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.570
most SOF, regardless of their service,

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.033
we're doing fundamentally
the same mission.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.700
That's no longer the case.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.670
We need undersea to be undersea.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.440
We need people in the
air to be in the air.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.980
We even now are starting to
have SOF aligned space operators

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.680
and we are like, the
whole spectrum is there,

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.750
and we need people to be
aligned to their service.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.510
And that again, tactical excellence,

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.550
has to be at the highest
level proficiency,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.130
so that we can create new effects.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.260
It is not about doing the same mission

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.410
and we have to build as an institution,

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.560
some institutional things that
we didn't really have before.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.077
And it's tricky I would say,

00:10:04.077 --> 00:10:05.520
and I think those of you who are operators

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.430
can speak to this, but when
you're doing sort of this,

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.350
a huge number of reps and
sets on the same mission,

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:13.380
passing tactical knowledge to
the next unit is fairly easy.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.660
You know, this unit comes off the field,

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:16.493
here's what we learned,

00:10:16.493 --> 00:10:18.840
here's the new state of play,
off to the next unit handoff.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.480
And it keeps getting better
and better and better.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.810
When you're doing a wide
array of different missions

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.600
in different domains and
bringing multi-domainS together,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.360
that learning process has
to become more systemic.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.330
It has to become more
institutionalized and thoughtful

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.400
and it still has to somehow be easy.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.040
And that's probably the
hardest part of this.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.700
And so we are looking at
really interesting technologies

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.680
to try to help us with
machine learning and AI

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.510
and how to enable the human even more,

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:46.680
because we have to do it,

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.350
not just for the tactical excellence,

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.780
but so that operationally we are learning

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:52.740
in these different spaces,

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.840
when there might be a gap of a year

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:56.880
between when you run one kind
of operation to the next,

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.730
or maybe two years, we
might have to do five other

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:01.710
different types of operations in between.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.060
And the same force will be asked to do

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.940
very different things, and
that's hard, obviously.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.430
I mean, tactical proficiency
partly comes from practice.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.740
And so that's gonna be a balancing act,

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.360
as we move into this new timeframe,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.000
where we have a really
unique array of missions

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.260
we think that we're
gonna be presented with,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.530
because again, we're
scalable, we're tailorable,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.070
we can do something that
gives our national leadership

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.710
options that they would
not otherwise have.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:26.880
And we can create dilemmas,

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.503
that might otherwise be obvious,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.770
who the creator of that dilemma is.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.320
And that's a goal of ours
every time we can do it.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.350
So we are doing this in different ways.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:41.310
One way that we're trying to
strengthen the institution is,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:42.660
we are really focusing on something

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.820
that the deputy secretary
is very eager for us to do,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.150
which is really build our
analytics and decision tools.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:49.650
And that sounds really boring and wonky

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.800
and I know that it kind
of is, but it's vital.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.840
I mean AI, machine learning
don't get you're much

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.420
if you're learning, you
know, how to tie your shoe

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:58.440
and you already knew how to do that,

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.030
like that's not really helpful.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:01.800
We need to learn how to do the things

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:02.760
that we don't do well today

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.770
or learn where we thought we
were good and it turns out

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:06.690
we weren't having the
impact we thought we were.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.060
So it's both about metrics and analytics

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:10.230
in improving our decision tools.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:14.100
We are investing a lot in
defendable, repeatable analytics

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.620
and decision tools now
as a SOF enterprise.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:19.170
And that's an exciting
place with war gaming,

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.950
bringing SOF into the
big service war games

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:24.540
and many of you are
familiar with, I think,

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:25.590
the challenge that we face,

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:28.650
which is most war gaming
starts at phase three.

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:31.590
So if our entire strategy rests
on a concept of deterring,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.647
which means we didn't
have the conflict right?

00:12:34.647 --> 00:12:36.360
Once we're at stage three,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.730
we're in the conflict,
where we have to prevail.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:39.870
We kind of lost,

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.240
we've failed in most of
our strategy at that point.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.120
Our goal is obviously to
win in the prevent piece,

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.570
but when it comes to prevail,

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:49.290
you only prevail if you've created

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.000
some asymmetric advantages going in.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.230
And that's where SOF can help.

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.000
That's where we can create, again,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.240
not just dilemmas, but now
advantages for our side.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:58.920
The joint force needs some of these

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.710
key asymmetric advantages
and SOF is positioned

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:04.510
to build those building blocks
that will give them that.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.880
But it's not gonna be
easy and it's challenging

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.000
as the entire national security
community and apparatus

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.510
grapples with what does it mean,

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:19.320
to intentionally compete and
try to avoid the conflict?

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.720
How much do we devote
to those lines of effort

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:23.310
and what is the shape of that?

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.530
That's an entire inter-agency effort.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.990
It's an entire national
effort to be honest.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.510
And I think we're still finding
our way a little bit there.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:31.860
And so that is one of our challenges,

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.200
because for SOF to plug in, you know,

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.430
we plug in, kind of again,

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.620
in phases sort of zero
to two, if you will.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.110
Once the shooting starts we have a roll,

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:41.610
and history is shown again,

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:44.583
no war goes off without SOF
ending up having to go in

00:13:44.583 --> 00:13:49.143
and create some of those key
advantages, key intelligence,

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:51.930
key strategic moments.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.200
But our focus is really on
everything that happens before

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:58.350
and building that into the
war games that we're doing

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:02.100
is also pushing the joint
force to think more seriously

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.960
about what beyond presence

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.330
and a credible military force option

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.940
they bring to the table in prevent.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.850
And it's again,

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:12.700
it's a challenge.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.560
So the other thing is as
we look at innovation,

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.510
and Bill's gonna talk about
this a lot more than I need to,

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:19.470
so I won't go too far in it,

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.570
but there's some really
exciting innovation,

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.340
besides AI and machine learning,

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.980
and kind of how we use data and you know,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:29.880
we have a lot of things the
commander likes to talk about

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.280
all of our nexts, we have ISR next,

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.620
we have next gen mobility,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:35.580
I think there's a few more next gen,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:38.850
we have a lot of nexts and that
again just reflects the fact

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:40.200
that we all see that where we are today

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.820
is not where we have to be.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.110
This is the decisive decade.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.990
So we have to invest
smartly across the spectrum

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:47.850
and probably the area that I personally,

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:49.740
am most interested in
and am pushing us out,

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:51.810
but that we're all doing it
together as an enterprise,

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:53.280
is the cognitive domain.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.530
I think there is so much
work we can do there.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.390
As we think about this whole spectrum

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.230
of the cognitive domain,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.800
it's both about what we do
to enhance our performance

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:03.750
as the elite performers.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:04.583
You know, we're sort of,

00:15:04.583 --> 00:15:06.300
we think of SOF almost as as your elite,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:07.530
you know, pro-athlete.

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:08.820
How do you enhance that performance

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.350
by using the cognitive domain,

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:15.000
but then how do you apply
that and how do you disable,

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.820
dismantle, decrement the
adversary's performance?

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.470
How do you get in the
adversary's decision loop?

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.630
That's classic, you
know, OODA loop theory,

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:24.780
that's not rocket science,
but it's a new enemy.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:26.370
How are we looking at that adversary

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.980
and understanding their
decision processes?

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.480
If you look around academia today,

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.850
I would say there's at
least 20 different theories

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.613
of the case for how the PRC
likes to make decisions.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.460
Which one's right? I don't know.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.710
But I am worried that we as an enterprise

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:42.150
have not been able to model

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:45.600
or really simulate enough of those options

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.280
to start to refine our understanding

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:49.440
of the dynamic that we have to be at,

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.270
where we test and we come back

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.280
and see what did they
do, what didn't they do?

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.870
We're still very early in those phases

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.240
and I think that is a
real challenge for us.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.073
So again,

00:15:58.073 --> 00:16:01.020
I'll let Bill talk more about
the specifics in innovation,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.480
but it is an exciting area where I think,

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.993
we believe we can enable
our humans and, you know,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.740
from the top to the bottom, I
think the one thing I can say

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.420
about the SOF community and enterprise,

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:14.610
is we all believe the
human is far more important

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:16.740
than the hardware or the enabling tech.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.510
It's about the innovative use of that,

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:19.680
that that human puts that to,

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.740
or the how you enable that
human to be more effective.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.390
But science is really moving rapidly

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:25.890
and if we can harness that and focus it,

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.080
I think we have tremendous opportunities.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.940
So again, I'll just go
back to where I started,

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:34.800
which is we are evolving
once again as SOF,

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.330
to meet the nation's challenges.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.190
I think it starts with
our tactical excellence

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:39.480
leading to strategic impact.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.280
And the first step in that

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.920
for us, is that underpinning to the NDS,

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:45.480
which is preventing the
strategic distraction,

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.820
so the joint force can modernize,

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.980
so that they can mobilize
the way they need to.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.350
But it's also in doing our core missions

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.880
and going back to our roots

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.790
across those three lines of
effort the NDS demands of us.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.250
And with that I'll take questions.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.870
- [Speaker] So for those
that want ask questions,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:01.800
please raise your hand.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:05.140
Also when you have mic, push
the button, it'll turn green,

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:07.350
you'll be able to project across the room.

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:08.750
Any questions that stand up?

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:14.073
Emma please, any questions?

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:19.440
Emma is one of our senior
advisor professors at Yale.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.600
- And feel free to ask,
I mean you can ask about

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:22.890
anything you want about, in terms of,

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:25.830
how things are in DC, what
my office actually does?

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:26.663
Not everyone knows that.

00:17:26.663 --> 00:17:29.110
I mean whatever questions
you have, I'm very open.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:32.197
- [Audience Member] I have a question.

00:17:32.197 --> 00:17:35.010
Thank you for your talk.

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.350
You mentioned AI,

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:40.920
and you mentioned AI in
the context of operations.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.170
Forgive my ignorance but like,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.810
how does that work?

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:48.123
- Yeah, so I think it comes down to,

00:17:49.373 --> 00:17:50.550
\what we see more and more,

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:53.400
which is information
overload, is one piece of it.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:58.400
So using AI to give you a
more digestible set of options

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:00.630
and then sort of assessing

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:01.800
some of the likelihood of those options,

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.900
'cuz there's a piece that
is war fighter experience.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.000
But again, as we go into this new era,

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:07.260
where you're not gonna
have the reps and sets

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:08.760
on the same mission set,

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.040
it's gonna be more important
to take different data sets

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:13.530
and create the options and
get a sense of your, you know,

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:16.263
you're still gonna rely
on war fighter kind of,

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:19.440
the thing that isn't science, the art,

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.060
but you're also gonna be informed now,

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:23.820
by what the AI tells you are
some of your better options

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.830
or some of the opportunities.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:29.610
It's also using the AI in much
bigger, back at, you know,

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:31.410
sort of headquarters
war gaming, hopefully,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.750
to more effectively model and simulate

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:35.670
and see where we can get.

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:36.750
I think those are probably two places,

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.900
and I know Bill, probably has
some other like thoughts too.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:40.733
- [Bill] Yes ma'am.

00:18:40.733 --> 00:18:42.870
I guess two things popped into my head is,

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:45.300
a lot of like next generation
ISR you talked about,

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.730
you know, processing all
that data that, you know,

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:49.470
we're collecting from an ISR perspective

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:50.670
is just huge amounts of data.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.410
So we definitely need more advanced tools

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:53.817
in that arena, and then.

00:18:53.817 --> 00:18:55.017
- And then machine, and then maintenance.

00:18:55.017 --> 00:18:57.360
- And the machine learning
part of it, yeah, and then,

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:59.760
you know, the other thing is,
you know, irregular warfare,

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:01.680
sentiment analysis, that
kind of thing, you know,

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.690
for our operators and, you
know, in foreign countries,

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:05.700
or you know, more hostile scenarios,

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.040
just having a better
understanding of the sentiment,

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.710
of the local population, if you will.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.990
I think that's another example
I think where AIML tools

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:14.730
could be useful,
especially using, you know,

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:16.410
publicly available information,

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:17.670
cyber tools and those kind of things.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.350
So just a couple other examples.

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:21.360
- Yeah and I would say like,
and the other thing is,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.550
improving kind of our
maintenance of our fleets.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:27.330
Like, there's a lot there
that we're actually,

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.970
with industry which is
advancing in that space a lot,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:31.860
where your sensors, along with like your,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.680
we have much more efficient
models now for how to use our,

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.720
even our fleets that as they get older,

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:38.580
we're more efficiently
employing them because of AI,

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.250
because we're able to see
specifically where they're broken

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:44.393
and not just rely on a
static profile of, you know,

00:19:44.393 --> 00:19:46.740
two missions and then you
get something, or you know,

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:47.850
it's just not static anymore.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.820
It's more specific to the actual aircraft

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.160
or other parts of our fleet.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:54.750
So that's another kind of big area.

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:56.850
And then we're trying to automate,

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.590
it's not just sort of sentiment analysis,

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:02.670
but the whole spectrum of
intelligence that we collect,

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:04.860
because again, it's
overwhelming in some cases,

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:07.950
in some opportunities, to
the degree that we can start

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:10.230
to automate pieces of that as we,

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.960
particularly in areas where
we're already a little clear

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.750
about the adversary that
would actually allow us

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:17.033
to free up humans to do some other things,

00:20:17.033 --> 00:20:18.810
so that you get a smaller number of humans

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.963
who have to do that exploitation
and analysis in some cases.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:23.873
Yeah.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.903
- [Sam] Good morning ma'am, Sam Hussein,

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:32.730
representing Peraton
Corporation from Washington.

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:35.790
Can you just touch a little bit more about

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.160
and tell us a little bit about

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.530
the Office of Strategic Capital,

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.860
that was just launched
recently at the Pentagon,

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:45.120
and how it's working out

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.690
as far as engaging in
bridging industry innovation

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.570
and bringing it to the SOF war fighters?

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.270
- I wish I could, but I cannot.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:56.333
So, Bill hopefully knows more about this.

00:20:56.333 --> 00:20:59.100
This is probably more
firmly in Bill's name. Yeah.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:00.180
- The only reason I jump on that ma'am,

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:01.230
is 'cuz they had something called

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.900
the Trusted Capital Marketplace
a couple of years ago,

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:05.760
that they were working and you know,

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:09.060
basically what it is is a,
you know, an analysis of,

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.373
you know, venture capitalist,

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:18.080
you know, different capital
sources to feed small businesses

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.760
and those kind of things
that might have capability

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:21.810
that we need.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:25.590
But definitely wanna make sure
that that funding is trusted,

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.510
you know, trying to build
that synergy between,

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.820
you know, the capital
and the small business

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.650
to what our operators need.

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:35.190
So that whole chain of security, you know,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:36.210
is the intent of that.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:40.020
So I don't think that the
Strategic Capital Office,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:41.940
I haven't seen anything
huge out of that yet,

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:43.320
but I think it's the same intent.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.490
That's not much of an answer.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.534
It's a little bit nascent
but that's the same intent.

00:21:47.534 --> 00:21:48.367
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

00:21:48.367 --> 00:21:49.560
- [Speaker] All right, we have
time for one more question

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.760
and it's gonna come from
Ms. Amasky in the back.

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:52.593
- Okay.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:55.073
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

00:21:55.073 --> 00:21:57.210
Good morning and thank you for coming.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:01.140
You mentioned cognitive ability.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.120
So my question is, you know,

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.700
you come to a university like Yale,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:07.950
how are you going to attract the brightest

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.590
and best students in the country

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.860
to want to pursue a
career in the military,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.530
let alone special forces?

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.560
- So it's an interesting challenge,

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.423
because I actually think we have,

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:27.630
so much of what we hear
America's youth wants today

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:30.150
and special forces actually
has it sort of in spades,

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:31.710
I would say.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:35.730
So when people want meaningful work,

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.690
they want a working environment where,

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:42.420
their boss and people care
about them as a whole person,

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.340
they want to make a difference,

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.350
they wanna be part of a team.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.530
All of that we offer, along
with healthcare benefits,

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:51.000
everything that you don't
get in the private sector.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.380
So in our world,

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.840
and we've actually like
really ramped this up even,

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.600
like NSW does this amazing thing,

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.370
where I think it's every six months now,

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.080
and it's only been about two
years you've been doing this,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.690
but like at every echelon level,

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.000
the commander has to meet
with their formation,

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.900
every single individual, with
themselves, the chaplain,

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:12.900
somebody else and somebody else
present, the doc, everybody,

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.030
to literally find out how are you doing,

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.700
specifically, tell me what's
going on in your life,

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.150
how's your family, how's your
finances, how's your health?

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.550
Are you feeling okay
mentally? Are you stressed?

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.670
And then from that you
get specific referrals

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:29.220
to whatever will help
make your life better.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:32.280
And there are no
disciplinary or professional

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:33.840
sort of career consequences.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:36.060
That happens every six
months at each echelon,

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:37.140
that has to happen.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:39.600
And so that investment in the person,

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:41.790
is something that I hear
young people craving,

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:43.170
somebody wanting to mentor them,

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.420
to grow them, to help
them be more effective,

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:49.291
to give them that support, to
have a community that cares.

00:23:49.291 --> 00:23:51.480
So I really think our
storyline is actually great.

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.910
And then, we also in special operations,

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:56.910
literally tackle the
hardest problems, right?

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.890
The wicked hard problems
are what we're trying to do,

00:23:58.890 --> 00:23:59.910
to be the best.

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:02.853
And so it actually I think is a little,

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:06.150
we struggle with I guess
the marketing of it,

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:08.970
because it's very clear it's
not working at this point.

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:11.130
But I think the story we
have to tell is fantastic.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:12.930
And if we could tell it more effectively

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:15.750
in the spaces where young people
are talking and listening,

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.512
I think we have a real opportunity.

00:24:17.512 --> 00:24:19.470
Now we do draw from the general force.

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:21.540
And I will say, I think
the other thing is,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.693
and we're talking about this
in the Pentagon, how do we,

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:30.960
modify, reinvigorate, revamp,
the contracts we make, right?

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:33.750
So if you're in the Army
and you come into the Army,

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:36.000
and what you really, really love to do

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:39.180
is sit at your computer
and do cyber operations

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:42.330
and you really don't like
marching through the woods,

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.910
you have zero interest
in the outdoors, right?

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.500
I still want your cyber talent.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:48.810
How do I build pathways where
you can come in and out?

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:50.760
Where you learn enough and understand,

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:52.290
you can't be a drain on the force.

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:53.880
But there's a lot of operations we now do,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:57.120
based here in the US that
enable our forward operators.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.580
How do we honestly admit that,

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.040
bring that into the force
successfully, and then grow that?

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:05.040
And so there is a challenge as
we sort of have to reimagine

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:06.930
some of the contracts we
make with America's youth,

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.240
because there's a piece that is absolutely

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:11.130
always gonna be about, you
have to be physically fit,

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:12.150
you have to be able to perform

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:15.630
in high stress environments forward.

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:17.430
But there is an a
growing piece of our work

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:18.600
that actually happens back here,

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.120
where there's still stress,
but it is very different.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:23.460
You don't necessarily need
exactly the same things

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.650
that I think people think you
need for the military career

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:26.850
to be successful.

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.090
And can we also make it so
that you can come in and out,

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:31.830
three years you're in giving me your best,

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.500
then you go out for three
years, you learn from industry,

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:36.000
you do the things you like to do there,

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.380
but then we can bring you back.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:38.430
Right now in some cases,

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.890
we have very arbitrary time limits on that

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:43.710
and age limits, that given
as we age as a population,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:44.543
as we're healthier,

00:25:44.543 --> 00:25:47.070
and as some of our jobs
really aren't that stressful.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:48.660
You know, people wouldn't have to like,

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:50.730
why do we say that's the
age you can't do it anymore

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:51.870
for the nation?

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:53.640
So I do think there's a little bit of,

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.290
there's conversations about that.

00:25:56.340 --> 00:25:59.460
That is an extremely
hard problem, I will say,

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:01.533
because I will also, I mean no surprise,

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:06.000
to produce the military that
we've produced over the years,

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.540
taking risk when you know one
thing works is really hard,

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.430
'cuz you're putting people's
lives on the line potentially.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:15.780
And so, big Army is gonna
have a hard, hard, hard time

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:17.130
reimagining this contract.

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:18.090
But they know they have to.

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:19.440
And so they're thinking about it,

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:20.580
they're talking about it.

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:22.320
Around the margins,
they're starting to do it.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:23.580
But I think that's the other piece of it.

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:24.930
I think we have a great story to tell,

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.750
but we also need our foundational services

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:28.950
to be able to tell a story

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:31.403
that's a little different
to America's youth, I think.

00:26:33.166 --> 00:26:33.999
Is that it?

00:26:33.999 --> 00:26:34.940
- [Speaker] All right,
with that, please join me

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.612
in thanking DAS E. Logan
for the presentation.

00:26:36.612 --> 00:26:38.222
(audience applause)

00:26:38.222 --> 00:26:40.805
(upbeat music)

